A safe state in operational semantics refers to a state where every thread has successfully completed its execution and obtained its desired result. It guarantees context switching safety — i.e., each thread gets its initial stack back and returns control to the OS kernel immediately after executing its code block. However, it doesn't guarantee memory protection or other service synchronization requirements.

Example: Consider a multi-threaded program with the following function:
```csharp
public static void MyFunction(ref int count) {
    // execute counting loop...
}
```
If `MyFunction` executes within a critical section, e.g.:
```c++
#include "pch.h"
using namespace std;
int main() {
   auto start = high_resolution_clock::now();
   myfunction(&count);
   auto end = high_ resolution_ clock:: now();
   cout << "time taken (" << duration_cast<seconds>(end - start)-> seconds () * 1000 + duration_ cast<microsecond >(start - end)[0] / 10 ^6 << endl;;
   return 0;
 }
 ```
Then calling this function inside a critical section will cause time travel and potentially trigger a deadlock due to shared data race. On the other hand, if the function runs outside any critical section, there won't be any deadlock since everything

A safe state in operational semantics refers to a state where every thread has successfully completed its execution and obtained its desired result. It guarantees context switching safety — i.e., each thread gets its initial stack back and returns control to the OS kernel. However, it doesn't guarantee memory protection or data consistency; these must be maintained within the CPU caches via shared cache management.

Example: Consider a multi-threaded program with the code:
```csharp
class Program {
    static void Main(string[] args) {
        Thread th = new Thread(() => {
            try {
                Console.WriteLine("Thread #1");
                sleep(); // non-atomic read/write
            } catch (Exception e) {
                throw new Exception($"Error in thread #{th.Id}:", e);
            } finally {
                ThrowIfFailed();
                Console._ReadLine().Dispose();
            }
        });

        th.Start();
        Thread t2 = new Thread() { IsBackground = true };
        t2.Name += "T#2";
        t.Join();
        t = null;
        t++;
        t?.BeginInvoke((MethodInvoker)(delegate () {
            while (!IsTerminated()) ;// lock here!

            try{

            }catch(...){}finally{}
